 "verdict": "CONTROL (the CHAOTIC init signature -- see the module docstring for why an earlier version of this check had the sign backwards): random init gives lambda +0.3598 with ignition 1.00 and D_norm 0.992, against Pythia's measured +0.3363 at ignition 1.00. It reproduces the chaotic init, as a randomly initialised model must. PRIMARY: THE DEVELOPMENTAL CURVE'S ENDPOINTS REPLICATE OUTSIDE PYTHIA. Init lands at +0.3598 against Pythia's +0.3363, and all 4 trained checkpoints land within 0.1 of Pythia's plateau +0.1683 (measured [0.1843, 0.1813, 0.1874, 0.189]), using dev_transition_phase3.measure imported unchanged -- same estimator, same geometry, only the family varies. lambda_ca across the OLMo-2 grid spans 0.1785 against its own noise floor 0.03864 (4.62x, gate 2.0x) BOUNDARY, REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN AND BINDING ON HOW THIS MAY BE WRITTEN. This generalises the PHENOMENON, not its TIMING, and the reason is the field's checkpoint supply rather than a design choice. Pythia-410m crosses within 0.034-1.07B tokens; OLMo-2's grid has one point at 1B and the next at 21B, so any crossing here is bracketed to a ~20B-wide interval -- forty times wider. OLMo-1B has ZERO checkpoints inside Pythia's window (earliest 4B, spacing 4B), TinyLlama publishes a single branch, and Amber is 7B parameters at ~3.6B spacing. Pythia is an outlier in early-checkpoint density, and 'is the transition at the same token count across families' is therefore NOT ANSWERABLE with public checkpoints today. That is a fact about what can be known, and it is the honest residue of this debt. ATTRIBUTION: tokenizer, architecture, corpus, data order and optimiser all differ from Pythia simultaneously. This is a generality test and cannot attribute any difference it finds to any one of them.",
